cy and a national climate favoring Democrats keep this from being a total foregone conclusion but leave her a clear underdog.", "watch": "Fresh polling showing Barr within single digits, or a surge of Democratic outside spending into the district, would be the first sign the national environment is outrunning the seat's partisan lean.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Jack Bergman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Michigan's 1st District covers the Upper Peninsula and much of the northern Lower Peninsula and leans heavily Republican. Republican incumbent Jack Bergman is seeking another term, while Democrat Callie Barr won her primary to set up a rematch of their 2024 contest. Nine expert ratings cover the race, and seven place it in the Safe or Solid Republican column, with the other two calling it Likely Republican.\n\nBergman has out-raised Barr more than two to one and holds a modest cash advantage, and the Detroit News has endorsed his reelection. No public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on the district's partisan lean, the incumbent's record, and the ratings consensus. A national climate favoring Democrats gives Barr some room, but she would need midterm dynamics to run far beyond normal in a seat this red.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0479, "margin": -11.4}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0291, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0616, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0563, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0486, "margin": -11.5}]}